\title{sciparseR: Provenance-Aware Scientific Document Parsing and Extraction}
\description{
\if{html}{\figure{logo.svg}{options: style='float: right' alt='logo' width='120'}}

Turns scientific PDF documents and 'JATS' XML into a structured, versioned document model with explicit source provenance. Born-digital PDF files are parsed from word positions with deterministic reading order reconstruction (line segmentation, recursive XY-cut with column detection, repeated header and footer detection, conservative hyphenation repair), sections, captions, footnotes, equations, figures, references and in-text citations. Tables are reconstructed as cells with row and column spans, header levels, missing value markers and source boxes. 'JATS' XML is a first class source whose semantic structure is preserved. A schema-driven extraction layer with a deterministic rules engine and an optional language model adapter extracts study design, population, intervention, comparator, outcome and numerical results, binding each number to its arm, outcome, time point, analysis set and effect measure. Every extracted value keeps an evidence record (page, section, table cell, exact text, character offsets, bounding box) and a status separating reported, derived, model inferred and human corrected data. Deterministic validators check counts, confidence intervals, ratios and p-values and flag contradictions between text and tables. Results export to 'JSON', 'Markdown', 'CSV', 'Parquet' and 'JATS'. Optical character recognition and 'ONNX' models are optional.
}
